    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 6,
      "bbox": [
        361,
        186,
        434,
        907
      ],
      "margin_number": 195,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "You have already met present infinitives (‘to – ’) formed by adding -ειν\n-εσθαι to the present stem (150). Greek also has aorist infinitives, formed\nby adding the appropriate endings to the aorist stem (first and second).\nTheir forms are as follows:",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "order": 7,
      "bbox": [
        450,
        186,
        467,
        452
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "First aorist infinitive active",
      "lang": "en",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "box",
      "order": 8,
      "bbox": [
        483,
        186,
        568,
        905
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "παῦσα-ι, ‘to stop’\nTo form the first aorist infinitive active, add -αι to the aorist stem, e.g\nἔ-παυσα, ‘I stopped’ > stem: παυσα- > παῦσα-ι, ‘to stop’",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
